2026-08-16T14:18:26Z    [DE:Diluted EPS (GAAP)] GATE critic: The stated weighted blend is miscomputed. Using your own inputs — 4.4027 at weight 0.928 and 6.75971 at weight 0.072 — the weighted mean is 4.4027×0.928 + 6.75971×0.072 = 4.0857056 + 0.48669912 = 4.57240472, not 4.57349. The proposed EPS is therefore inconsistent with its derivation and is slightly overstated (~0.001085/share).
2026-08-16T14:18:38Z    [HD:Net sales] GATE critic: You scaled FY2025Q2’s $45.3bn by +4.86% to get FY2026Q2, but that +4.86% appears to be the comparable-sales (like‑for‑like) change, not the reported net sales growth. Using a comp rate to project total net sales is a basis error. For FY2025Q2, Home Depot’s release reports both “comparable sales increased X%” and “sales increased to $45.3 billion, up Y%,” and X and Y are not the same. The projection should use the reported net sales YoY growth, not comps.
2026-08-16T14:18:52Z    [DE:Production & Precision Ag operating profit] GATE critic: The source evidence includes “FY2025: $17,311,” which is almost certainly the Production & Precision Ag segment’s net sales (USDm), not operating profit. Mixing a revenue figure into an operating-profit forecast is a wrong-basis error; it’s also inconsistent with the cited FY2025Q3 operating profit of $580m (a full-year operating profit of $17,311m would be nonsensical).
2026-08-16T14:18:52Z  [DE] wrote submission\DE-FY2026Q3.xlsx
2026-08-16T14:18:52Z  [DE] wrote research\DE.md
2026-08-16T14:19:02Z    [HAS:Net fees] GATE critic: Mixing bases: the -12.68% YoY you applied for FY2024→FY2025 is a like‑for‑like/constant‑currency change, but you applied it to the reported FY2025 net fees base of £972.4m (GBPm). Using an LFL percentage on a reported‑GBP base yields a FY2026 figure on the wrong basis. This can be checked against Hays’ FY2025 results, which show c.£972.4m reported net fees and an LFL change (≈‑12%) that differs from the reported % change.
2026-08-16T14:19:17Z    [HD:Adjusted diluted EPS] GATE critic: Wrong period carry-forward: Your only cited figure is HD’s adjusted diluted EPS for FY2025Q2 = $4.68, and your FY2026Q2 value of 4.68245 is essentially the same (≈0.0% change). That indicates you’ve effectively carried forward the prior-year quarter’s number rather than forecasting FY2026Q2. This is a concrete period error (“prior-year figure carried forward by mistake”) and can be checked against HD’s FY2025Q2 adjusted EPS disclosure ($4.68) versus the proposed FY2026Q2 $4.68245.
2026-08-16T14:19:29Z    [HAS:Pre-exceptional basic EPS] GATE guidance: 1.32445 is 97.1% away from the guided midpoint 45 with no explicit range to justify it
2026-08-16T14:19:29Z    [HAS:Pre-exceptional basic EPS] GATE critic: You derived EPS using a 35.1% tax rate, but the company guides to an FY2026 effective tax rate of about 45%. Using the guided FY26 ETR on the same PBT (45.85−13.4=£32.45m) and 1,590.2m shares gives c. £17.85m net income and ~1.12 GBp EPS, not 1.324 GBp. The proposed value is on the wrong tax basis for FY26 and is overstated.
2026-08-16T14:19:51Z    [HAS:Pre-exceptional operating profit] GATE critic: You overrode the company’s stated FY2026 guidance/consensus. Hays says “FY26 pre-exceptional operating profit is expected to be in line with market consensus expectations of £45.2m,” yet you used an unsupported “guidance_anchor” of 46.3884 and blended it with a seasonal trend to submit 45.8522 GBPm. The forecast should align to the £45.2m FY2026 consensus figure in GBPm, not exceed it.
